// The primitive string type.
//
// `str` is a **borrowed, UTF-8 view** — the same thing Rust's `str` is. It is
// `distinct []u8`: identical in representation to a byte slice (a pointer and a
// byte length), and a different type on purpose.
//
// Why distinct rather than a plain `[]u8` (§2.4):
//
//   - The UTF-8 invariant needs somewhere to live. If every `[]u8` were a `str`,
//     `chars()` on arbitrary bytes would either validate on every call or read
//     garbage.
//   - Slicing needs a hook. `s[0..2]` on `"héllo"` cuts the `é` in half;
//     `distinct` gives that check a place to happen.
//   - Text wants operations bytes do not — `split`, `trim`, `chars` — and they
//     have to hang off something that is not every `[]u8`.
//   - `[]u8` should display as `[104, 105]` and `str` as `hi`. One type can only
//     have one impl.
//
// Being `distinct []u8` and not a compiler primitive means all the slice
// machinery — interior pointers, bounds, GC tracing — is inherited rather than
// reimplemented. The compiler finds this type by its `#lang` tag, which is how a
// string literal knows what type it has.
//
// The **owned**, growable string is not here and will not be: it belongs to
// `std`, built on top of this one, and will carry its own `#lang` tag so that a
// `str` coerces into it implicitly.

{ Eq } :: import "../cmp.nest"
{ bytes_eq } :: import "../iter/slice.nest"

@public str :: #lang("str") distinct []u8

// `len` is deliberately absent: a `distinct` type inherits the methods of the
// type it is distinct from (§2.4), so `s.len()` already resolves to `[]T`'s and
// gives the length in **bytes**. That is the right answer — the byte length is
// the O(1) one, and it differs from the character count for any text outside
// ASCII. The inheritance is one-way, so nothing here leaks back onto `[]u8`.
impl str {
  // The underlying bytes. Going this way is always sound: valid UTF-8 is a
  // subset of arbitrary bytes. The reverse needs a check, and lives in `std`
  // until there is enough machinery here to write it.
  as_bytes :: #inline func (self: str) -> []u8 { return cast.<[]u8>(self) }
}

// Text compares by its **bytes**, not by where they are. Two `str`s cut out of
// different buffers are equal when they spell the same thing, which is the only
// answer `==` can usefully have for a borrowed view — the alternative compares a
// pointer and a length and calls two copies of `"hi"` different.
//
// It is the same `bytes_eq` a `"..."` **pattern** calls, so `s == "hi"` and
// `s.match { "hi" => ... }` cannot disagree.
impl Eq for str {
  eq :: func (self: Self, rhs: Self) -> bool {
    return bytes_eq(self.as_bytes(), rhs.as_bytes())
  }
}
